* DICT_FORM
* A dictionary item as a full-screen form: a label and an entry for each field its type has (S.90).
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
* START-HISTORY:
* 3 Nov 26 dm   S.123: the AI line's hint and the "no Type" refusal are messages 15006 and 15007 (Linux's second block).
* 3 Nov 26 dm   S.97, THE FORM'S AI LINE (the owner's D4a of 20 Oct: "a one line ai entry field at the bottom of each form"; 1 Nov: build dictionary items from a prompt): when the AI is
*               usable for the account ($tk_ai 'usable') the form has an AI> row at its foot, reached by F6, Tab or a click; Return in it asks $tk_ai, which shows everything that
*               will be sent first (Cancel the default).  The model is offered the form's fields (actx<14>) and may propose values; they are shown in full with Accept and Reject
*               (Reject the default), and only what passes this form's own rules (!dict_item) goes into the rows.  NOTHING IS SAVED: Accept of the form and the IDE's own save do that.
*               The item's values go in the request, so the caller says where the item is (the new argument org, actx<13>), and the AI's wall refuses an item of SD's own files.
* 1 Nov 26 dm   A Type typed over an existing item's blanked the form: type.left read the Type into cv, then collect (which reads every row into cv) ran before vals{'type'} = cv, so the
*               new type was the last row's value and the form was made again with no type.  The typed Type is kept in tl.ty across the collect (sandbox-dictform.py DF5a).
* 31 Oct 26 dm  THE LAYOUT THE OWNER SPECIFIED (his message of 31 Oct, after seeing the first build): Type is an ENTRY, typed, and the rows of that type appear when it has been entered
*               (a new item shows only Type until then); no row numbers; the labels Field #, Conversion Cd, Column Heading and ONE row Len & Just (30 L, 12 R 2) in place of
*               Width, Justification and Decimals; the buttons are Accept and Cancel and nothing else (Edit as text is gone: Cancel leaves the item as text in its tab).  The first
*               build had a radio row for the Type, numbered rows, three format rows and a third button.
* 31 Oct 26 dm  New (S.90, plan .claude/plan-s90-dict-form.md).  The rules are !dict_item's; this program is the screen.
* 8 Nov 26 dm  S.176: F6 / Shift-F6 go from the rows to the AI line and back (the box keys of every page); Tab still moves from row to row.
* 9 Nov 26 dm  S.186: what the program prints is in US spelling (the owner: "Everything SD prints"); its text only, no change of behaviour.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*   call $dict_form(rec, title, newrec, res, org)
*     rec     the dictionary record as it is ('' for a new item)
*     title   what the form's border says (the file and the item)
*     org     where the item is, for the AI's wall (3 Nov 26): record VM file VM id VM 1, as $tk_ai's actx<13> wants it.  A caller that gives none has an AI line that sends nothing
*             (the wall refuses a text whose origin cannot be told)
*     newrec  the record the form built, when res is save; the reason in words when res is cannot; else rec
*     res     save    Accept was pressed and every field passed: newrec is the record
*             cancel  Esc or Cancel (after the question when something was changed): nothing is returned
*             cannot  the form cannot show the item (a type it does not know, a format that is not a width, L R T or C and decimals, text with
*                     characters it cannot hold): newrec says why in words and the caller shows the item as text
*   A FULL-SCREEN FORM, 140 by 43 (the window is at least that: the IDE refuses a smaller one).  The first row is the Type, an entry: D, E, I, L, PH or X is typed and checked as the
*   focus leaves it, and the rows of that type appear (a new item shows nothing else until then; an existing item shows its rows with its values in them, to be edited).  Typing
*   another type makes the form again with the rows of that type and keeps what was typed for the rows the two types share.  Each other row is a label and an entry; a phrase or a text is
*   a text area; the format is ONE row, Len & Just, read as a width, a letter and optional decimals.  A value is checked as the focus leaves it, with the reason on the line near the foot, and
*   once more when Accept is pressed, which names the row and puts the focus there; nothing is returned while a row is refused.  The rules are those of !dict_item.
*   This program WRITES NOTHING: it opens no file and runs no command.  The caller (the IDE) puts the record in the tab and saves it with its own save.
*   THE AI LINE (3 Nov 26), only when the AI is usable for the account: the last row of the form, AI>.  Return in it asks the AI ($tk_ai 'ask': the preview of everything sent, Cancel
*   the default); the request carries what the form is, the item's values as they are on the screen and the form's fields (actx<14>); the model may propose values, which are shown in
*   full (Reject the default); Accept puts into the rows each value that passes !dict_item's own check, and says which did not.  The form then waits as before: Accept of the form and
*   the IDE's save are still the person's.  Follow up (on the answer's box) keeps the exchange for the next request typed here.
* END-DESCRIPTION
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine dict_form(rec, title, newrec, res, org)
$catalog $dict_form

$include sl.h
$include tk.h

   * the AI line (3 Nov 26): only when the AI is usable for the account (the gate, a provider, a model and a key)
   call $tk_ai('usable', '', ai.on, ai.pm)
   ai.keep = ''                            ;* the request typed in the line, kept across a form made again
   fm.note = ''                            ;* what the AI line has to say on the err row of the next form made
   fm.lastrow = ''                         ;* S.176: the row that had the focus before F6 took it to the AI line
   hn = ''                                 ;* the row with the focus (F6 may come before the first focus event)

   * the foot's keys: the form's own, as the IDE and the pages name theirs (the row is made by $tk_fkeys from this table)
   fm.keys = ''
   call $tk_keys('add', fm.keys, 'enter', 'accept', 'accept', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', fm.keys, 'esc', 'cancel', 'cancel', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', fm.keys, 'tab', 'next row', 'next', '', 1)
   call $tk_keys('add', fm.keys, 'shift-tab', 'previous row', 'previous', '', 1)
   if ai.on then call $tk_keys('add', fm.keys, 'f6', 'box', 'nextpane', '', 1)    ;* S.176: the rows and the AI line, F6 / Shift-F6 as on every page

   res = 'cancel'
   newrec = rec
   if rec = '' then
      call !dict_item('parse', 'D', vals, '', pd)
      vals{'type'} = ''                    ;* a new item has no type until one is typed
      vals{'width'} = '10'
      vals{'just'} = 'L'
      vals{'sm'} = 'S'
      is.new = 1
   end else
      call !dict_item('parse', rec, vals, '', pd)
      is.new = 0
      if pd # '' then
         res = 'cannot'
         begin case
            case pd = 'type'
               newrec = 'its type is not D, E, I, L, PH or X'
            case pd = 'format'
               newrec = 'its format is not a width, L, R, T or C, and decimals'
            case 1
               newrec = 'its text has characters the form cannot hold'
         end case
         return
      end
   end
   fmt.err = ''
   focus.first = 0
   focus.ai = 0

   loop
      gosub make.form
      gosub run.form
   until act # 'type' and act # 'ai' repeat
   return

* ======================================================================
* In: vals (the values).  Out: the form f, shown by the first screen wait
make.form:
   ty = vals{'type'}
   if ty = '' then
      rows = 'type' : @vm : 'Type' : @vm : 'type' : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 0
   end else
      call !dict_item('layout', ty, rows, '', xd)
      gosub fold.format
   end
   nrows = dcount(rows, @fm)
   * the body of the screen as the IDE lays it out (row 0 the bar, the form from row 1, then the status line, the border and the keys on the last three rows), so the form sits in
   * the same frame as the IDE and the pages and covers none of it: the window is never under 140 by 43 (the IDE refuses a smaller one)
   call $sl_window(win.w, win.h)
   fm.w = win.w
   if fm.w < 140 then fm.w = 140
   fm.h = win.h
   if fm.h < 43 then fm.h = 43
   ystat = fm.h - 3
   fh = ystat - 1
   y.hint = fh - 9
   y.err = fh - 7
   y.btn = fh - 4
   y.ai = fh - 2
   screen form title title : (if is.new then '  (new item)' else '') at 0,1 size fm.w,fh returning f
   screen label f, 'ttl', 'Type D, E, I, L, PH or X and press Tab: the rows of that type appear.  Tab and Down: next row.  Return: Accept.  Esc: leave.' at 2,1 width fm.w - 6
   y = 3
   for rp = 1 to nrows
      key = rows<rp, 1>
      screen label f, 'l.' : key, ' ' at 2,y width 22
      screen set f, 'l.' : key text rows<rp, 2> : ':'          ;* SET TEXT, not the label's own text: SCREEN LABEL takes the first & of its text for a hot key and drops it, and Len & Just has one
      begin case
         case key = 'type'
            screen entry f, 'type' at 26,y width 6 value vals{'type'} maxlen 2
         case rows<rp, 3> = 'memo'
            screen memo f, key, convert(char(10), @fm, vals{key}) at 26,y size 106,14
            y += 13
         case key = 'fmt'
            call !dict_item('fmtshow', vals, fx, '', xd)
            screen entry f, 'fmt' at 26,y width 14 value fx maxlen 12
         case 1
            gosub entry.size
            screen entry f, key at 26,y width ew value vals{key} maxlen em
      end case
      y += 2
   next rp
   screen label f, 'hint', ' ' at 2,y.hint width fm.w - 6
   screen label f, 'err', ' ' at 2,y.err width fm.w - 6
   screen button f, 'accept', '&Accept' at 26,y.btn default
   screen button f, 'cancel', '&Cancel' at 40,y.btn cancel
   if ai.on then
      * 3 Nov 26 (S.97): the AI line, the form's last row; Return in it asks the AI (on.button sees where the focus was), F6 puts the focus there
      screen label f, 'ailab', sysmsg(12641) at 2,y.ai width 22
      screen entry f, 'ai' at 26,y.ai width fm.w - 32 value ai.keep maxlen 300
   end
   if focus.first then
      if nrows >= 2 then screen focus f, rows<2, 1> else null
      focus.first = 0
   end
   if focus.ai then
      screen focus f, 'ai' else null
      focus.ai = 0
   end
   if fm.note # '' then
      screen set f, 'err' text fm.note[1, fm.w - 6]
      fm.note = ''
   end
   gosub draw.foot
   return

* The bar on row 0 (the form's name and the app's, as the pages' bar names the app at its right end) and the status line, the border and the row of keys on the last three rows: the
* frame of the IDE and the pages.  Written once for each form that is made; the cursor is put back where the focus left it (SCREEN TEXT leaves it after the text)
draw.foot:
   keep.cur = ''
   call $sl_curat('get', keep.cur)
   bar.r = 'dict.form'
   bar.t = ' Dictionary item ' : title : (if is.new then '  (new item)' else '')
   bar.t = fmt(bar.t[1, fm.w - len(bar.r) - 2], (fm.w - len(bar.r) - 1) : 'L') : bar.r : ' '
   screen text bar.t at 0,0 attr 'bar'
   st.t = '[Form]  ' : title : '   The rows are checked as you leave them.  Accept writes nothing itself: the IDE saves.'
   screen text fmt(st.t[1, fm.w], fm.w : 'L') at 0,ystat attr 'reverse'
   call $sl_line('h', 0, fk.hc)
   screen text str(fk.hc, fm.w) at 0,ystat + 1
   fk.ctx = ''
   fk.ctx<TK.C.W> = fm.w
   call $tk_fkeys(fk.ctx, fm.keys, '', fk.text)
   screen text fk.text at 0,ystat + 2 attr 'bar'
   call $sl_curat('back', keep.cur)
   return

* The engine's Width, Justification and Decimals rows are ONE row on the form, Len & Just (key fmt)
fold.format:
   nr2 = ''
   for rp = 1 to dcount(rows, @fm)
      k = rows<rp, 1>
      begin case
         case k = 'width'
            nr2<-1> = 'fmt' : @vm : 'Len & Just' : @vm : 'fmt' : @vm : '' : @vm : '' : @vm : 1
         case k = 'just' or k = 'dec'
            null
         case 1
            nr2<-1> = rows<rp>
      end case
   next rp
   rows = nr2
   return

* In: key.  Out: ew (the width of its entry), em (the longest text it takes)
entry.size:
   ew = 100
   em = 300
   begin case
      case key = 'loc'
         ew = 8 ; em = 5
      case key = 'sm'
         ew = 4 ; em = 1
      case key = 'conv'
         ew = 40 ; em = 40
      case key = 'head'
         ew = 44 ; em = 40
      case key = 'assoc'
         ew = 34 ; em = 30
      case key = 'file'
         ew = 40 ; em = 63
   end case
   return

* ======================================================================
* The form is on the screen between waits.  Out: act ('type': make the form again, 'done'), and res, newrec
run.form:
   act = ''
   loop
      screen wait f returning ev then
         begin case
            case ev<1> = 'focus'
               gosub on.focus
            case ev<1> = 'button'
               gosub on.button
            case ev<1> = 'key' and (ev<3> = 'f6' or ev<3> = 'shift-f6') and ai.on
               * S.176: F6 / Shift-F6 are the box keys of every page: the form's rows are one box and the AI line the other (a move the program makes is no
               *   focus event, so hn is set here); Tab still goes from row to row
               if hn = 'ai' then
                  if fm.lastrow = '' then fm.lastrow = 'type'
                  screen focus f, fm.lastrow else null
                  hn = fm.lastrow
               end else
                  screen focus f, 'ai' else null
                  hn = 'ai'
               end
               hint = ''
               gosub hint.for
               screen set f, 'hint' text hint
         end case
      end else
         if ev<1> = 'cancel' then gosub ask.discard
         if act = '' then
            if ev<1> # 'cancel' then
               res = 'cancel'
               act = 'done'
            end
         end
      end
      if act # '' then exit
   repeat
   screen hide f
   return

* The values of the rows on the form, into vals (the Type is vals{'type'} already).  Len & Just is split into width, justification and decimals; what is wrong with it is in fmt.err
collect:
   fmt.err = ''
   for rp = 1 to nrows
      key = rows<rp, 1>
      if key # 'type' then
         screen get f, key returning cv
         if rows<rp, 3> = 'memo' then cv = convert(@fm, char(10), cv)
         if key = 'fmt' then
            call !dict_item('fmtsplit', cv, fw, '', fe)
            if fe = '' then
               vals{'width'} = fw{'width'}
               vals{'just'} = fw{'just'}
               vals{'dec'} = fw{'dec'}
            end else
               fmt.err = fe
            end
         end else
            vals{key} = cv
         end
      end
   next rp
   return

* Esc or Cancel: nothing to ask when nothing was changed (or no Type was ever entered), else Discard or Keep editing.  Sets act and res when the form ends
ask.discard:
   if ty = '' then
      res = 'cancel'
      act = 'done'
      return
   end
   gosub collect
   call !dict_item('build', vals, rec, nr, be)
   if be = '' and nr = rec then
      res = 'cancel'
      act = 'done'
      return
   end
   screen msgbox 'Leave without saving the changes?' title 'Dictionary item' buttons '&Discard,&Keep editing' default 2 returning bq else bq = 1
   if bq = 1 then
      res = 'cancel'
      act = 'done'
   end
   return

* The focus moved: the row it left is checked and the hint of the row it went to is shown.  In: ev (the widget it went to, then the one it left)
on.focus:
   lfk = ev<3>
   hn = ev<2>
   if hn # 'ai' then fm.lastrow = hn                 ;* S.176: the row F6 comes back to from the AI line
   if lfk = 'type' then
      gosub type.left
   end else
      gosub row.left
   end
   if act = '' then
      hint = ''
      gosub hint.for
      screen set f, 'hint' text hint
   end
   return

* The Type entry was left (or Accept was pressed with a Type typed): a good type that is not the one on the form makes the form again with that type's rows, the focus on the first of them
type.left:
   screen get f, 'type' returning cv
   call !dict_item('check', 'D', 'type', cv, ce)
   if ce = '' then
      screen set f, 'type' value cv
      screen set f, 'err' text ' '
      if cv # ty then
         tl.ty = cv                         ;* collect reads every row into cv, so the Type typed is kept apart
         gosub collect
         vals{'type'} = tl.ty
         focus.first = 1
         act = 'type'
      end
   end else
      screen set f, 'err' text '! Type: ' : ce
      screen focus f, 'type' else null
   end
   return

* A row other than the Type was left: it is checked, and a good value is put back in its normal form
row.left:
   for rp = 1 to nrows
      if rows<rp, 1> = lfk and rows<rp, 3> # 'memo' then
         screen get f, lfk returning cv
         if lfk = 'fmt' then
            call !dict_item('fmtsplit', cv, fw, '', ce)
            if ce = '' then
               call !dict_item('fmtshow', fw, fx, '', xd)
               screen set f, 'fmt' value fx
            end
         end else
            call !dict_item('check', ty, lfk, cv, ce)
            if ce = '' then screen set f, lfk value cv
         end
         if ce = '' then
            screen set f, 'err' text ' '
         end else
            screen set f, 'err' text '! ' : rows<rp, 2> : ': ' : ce
         end
         exit
      end
   next rp
   return

* In: hn (a row key).  Out: hint
hint.for:
   begin case
      case hn = 'type'
         hint = 'D a data field, E an element of a collection, I a computed value, L a link to another file, PH a phrase, X text.  Type one and press Tab.'
      case hn = 'loc'
         hint = 'A whole number from 0 to 9999: the field of the record this item shows.  0 is the record id.'
      case hn = 'path'
         hint = 'The element of a collection record: names and array positions separated by /'
      case hn = 'expr'
         hint = 'One expression in SD BASIC, for example AMT * 2 or UPCASE(NAME)'
      case hn = 'idexpr'
         hint = 'An expression that gives the id of the record in the other file.  One expression, no semicolon.'
      case hn = 'file'
         hint = 'The file whose dictionary the linked names are looked up in'
      case hn = 'fexpr'
         hint = 'Optional: an expression that gives the name of the file actually read'
      case hn = 'conv'
         hint = 'Optional: a conversion for display, for example D2 for a date or MD2 for two decimals'
      case hn = 'head'
         hint = 'Optional: the column heading, up to 40 characters.  A backslash prints none.'
      case hn = 'fmt'
         hint = 'The width of the column, then L left, R right, T text that wraps or C centered, then decimals if any: 30 L or 12 R 2'
      case hn = 'sm'
         hint = 'S for one value in the field, M for many values (multivalued)'
      case hn = 'assoc'
         hint = 'Optional, used only when Single/Multi is M: a name shared by multivalued items whose values line up'
      case hn = 'user'
         hint = 'Optional: free for your application.  SD does not read it.'
      case hn = 'text'
         hint = 'The text, one line to a field.  A phrase is one line.'
      case hn = 'ai'
         hint = sysmsg(15006)                          ;* 3 Nov 26 (S.123): the form's AI hint, a message
   end case
   return

* A button: Accept builds the record from the rows; a row that fails is named in the err line and takes the focus; nothing ends the form while one fails
on.button:
   bt = ev<2>
   if bt = 'accept' and ai.on then
      * 3 Nov 26: Return while the AI line has the focus presses the form's default button; it is the AI's request, not the form's Accept (a click on Accept moves the focus to it first)
      fq.i = f{'focus'} + 0
      if fq.i > 0 then
         fq.w = f{'widgets', fq.i}
         if fq.w{'id'} = 'ai' then
            gosub ask.ai
            return
         end
      end
   end
   if bt = 'accept' then
      gosub type.left
      if act # '' then return
      if ty = '' then
         screen set f, 'err' text '! Type: D, E, I, L, PH or X'
         screen focus f, 'type' else null
         return
      end
      gosub collect
      if fmt.err # '' then
         screen set f, 'err' text '! Len & Just: ' : fmt.err
         screen focus f, 'fmt' else null
         return
      end
      call !dict_item('build', vals, rec, nr, be)
      if be = '' then
         newrec = nr
         res = 'save'
         act = 'done'
      end else
         fk = be<1>
         if fk = 'width' or fk = 'just' or fk = 'dec' then fk = 'fmt'
         screen set f, 'err' text '! ' : be<2>
         screen focus f, fk else null
      end
   end
   return

* ======================================================================
* THE AI LINE (3 Nov 26, S.97).  $tk_ai does the asking (the wall, the preview of everything sent with Cancel the default, the wait, the answer and Accept or Reject); this form says
* what it is, the item's values as they are on the screen and the fields the model may fill, and puts into its rows only what passes its own rules.  It writes nothing.  The form is
* made again afterwards (act 'ai'), because the boxes were drawn over it; what was typed in the rows is kept (collect), and the focus goes back to the line.
ask.ai:
   screen get f, 'ai' returning ai.req
   ai.keep = ai.req
   if trim(ai.req) = '' then return
   gosub collect
   ty.now = vals{'type'}
   page = 'This is the dictionary item form of SD Core for Linux: one item of the dictionary of a file, a row for each field its type has.'
   page<1, -1> = 'The item: ' : title : (if is.new then ' (a new item, not saved yet)' else '') : '.'
   if ty.now = '' then
      page<1, -1> = 'No Type has been entered yet: the form shows only the Type row.'
   end else
      page<1, -1> = 'Its Type is ' : ty.now : '.  What its rows hold now:'
      for rp = 1 to nrows
         key = rows<rp, 1>
         if key # 'type' then
            if key = 'fmt' then
               call !dict_item('fmtshow', vals, cv, '', xd)
            end else
               cv = vals{key}
            end
            page<1, -1> = '  ' : key : ' (' : rows<rp, 2> : '): ' : (if cv = '' then '(empty)' else change(cv, char(10), ' / '))
         end
      next rp
   end
   page<1, -1> = "A value the person accepts is put into the row only if it passes the form's own check; the person then accepts the form and the IDE saves it."
   flds = 'type: D a data field, E an element of a collection record, I a computed value, L a link to another file, PH a phrase, X text'
   flds<1, -1> = 'loc: Field # (D), a whole number from 0 to 9999; 0 is the record id'
   flds<1, -1> = 'path: Element Path (E), names and array positions separated by /'
   flds<1, -1> = 'expr: Expression (I), one SD BASIC expression'
   flds<1, -1> = 'idexpr: Id Expression (L), one expression that gives the id of the record in the other file'
   flds<1, -1> = 'file: File (L), the file whose dictionary the linked names are looked up in'
   flds<1, -1> = 'fexpr: File Expression (L), optional, an expression that gives the name of the file read'
   flds<1, -1> = 'conv: Conversion Cd (D E I), optional, for example D2 for a date or MD2 for two decimals'
   flds<1, -1> = 'head: Column Heading (D E I), optional, up to 40 characters'
   flds<1, -1> = 'fmt: Len & Just (D E I), the width, then L R T or C, then decimals if any: 30 L or 12 R 2'
   flds<1, -1> = 'sm: Single/Multi (D E I), S or M'
   flds<1, -1> = 'assoc: Association (D E I), only when Single/Multi is M, up to 30 letters, digits, dots and dashes'
   flds<1, -1> = 'user: User, optional, free for the application'
   flds<1, -1> = 'text: the Phrase (PH, one line) or the Text (X, lines separated by line feeds)'
   ai.org = org
   if ai.org = '' then ai.org = 'unknown'                  ;* the item's values are in the request: with no origin the wall refuses (fail shut)
   actx = 'dict.form' : @fm : 'form' : @fm : page : @fm : '' : @fm : 0 : @fm : ai.req : @fm : ystat
   actx<11> = 'nocmd'                                      ;* the form has nowhere to run a command: one from the model is ignored
   actx<13> = ai.org
   actx<14> = flds
   ai.ctx = ''
   ai.ctx<TK.C.W> = fm.w
   ai.res = ''
   call $tk_ai('ask', ai.ctx, actx, ai.res)
   fm.note = ai.res<5>
   begin case
      case ai.res<1> = 'fields'
         gosub apply.fields
         ai.keep = ''
      case ai.res<1> = 'follow'
         ai.keep = ''                                      ;* the line is empty for the follow-up question
   end case
   focus.ai = 1
   act = 'ai'
   return

* The values the person accepted (ai.res<2> the keys, ai.res<3> the values, as values) go into vals, each only if it passes !dict_item: the Type first (a good Type that differs
* makes the form of that type, as typing it does), then every other key that is a row of the item's type.  What is left out, and why, is said on the err row.
apply.fields:
   ak = ai.res<2>
   av = ai.res<3>
   an = dcount(ak, @vm)
   a.bad = ''
   a.ok = 0
   ty.new = vals{'type'}
   for i = 1 to an
      if ak<1, i> = 'type' then
         cv = trim(av<1, i>)
         call !dict_item('check', 'D', 'type', cv, ce)
         if ce = '' then
            ty.new = cv
            a.ok += 1
         end else
            a.bad<-1> = 'Type: ' : ce
         end
      end
   next i
   if ty.new = '' then
      if an > a.ok then a.bad<-1> = sysmsg(15007)
   end else
      call !dict_item('layout', ty.new, lr, '', xd)
      for i = 1 to an
         key = ak<1, i>
         cv = av<1, i>
         lp = -1                                           ;* the Type was taken above
         if key # 'type' then gosub apply.one
      next i
   end
   vals{'type'} = ty.new
   fm.note = 'The AI filled ' : a.ok : ' row' : (if a.ok = 1 then '' else 's') : '; check them, then Accept: nothing is saved until the IDE saves.'
   if a.bad # '' then fm.note := '  Not filled: ' : change(a.bad, @fm, '; ')
   return

* One key and its value (key, cv) against the rows of the type (lr): into vals when it passes, else a reason in a.bad
apply.one:
   lp = 0
   for rp = 1 to dcount(lr, @fm)
      if lr<rp, 1> = key or (key = 'fmt' and lr<rp, 1> = 'width') then
         lp = rp
         exit
      end
   next rp
   begin case
      case lp = 0 or key = 'width' or key = 'just' or key = 'dec'
         a.bad<-1> = key : ' is not a row of a ' : ty.new : ' item'
      case key = 'fmt'
         call !dict_item('fmtsplit', cv, fw, '', ce)
         if ce = '' then
            vals{'width'} = fw{'width'}
            vals{'just'} = fw{'just'}
            vals{'dec'} = fw{'dec'}
            a.ok += 1
         end else
            a.bad<-1> = 'Len & Just: ' : ce
         end
      case lr<lp, 3> = 'memo'
         vals{key} = cv                                    ;* a text area: its lines are kept; Accept of the form checks it with the rest
         a.ok += 1
      case index(cv, char(10), 1)
         a.bad<-1> = lr<lp, 2> : ': one line only'
      case 1
         call !dict_item('check', ty.new, key, cv, ce)
         if ce = '' then
            vals{key} = cv
            a.ok += 1
         end else
            a.bad<-1> = lr<lp, 2> : ': ' : ce
         end
   end case
   return
end

